Our tool-using plugins work by having a skill tell the model to run a CLI through the shell. That route has cost us repeatedly:
- script paths that only resolve inside Claude Code ([#118](https://github.com/lokkju/skills/issues/118), and the OpenCode path hooks in [#136](https://github.com/lokkju/skills/pull/136));
- `allowed-tools` pre-approval per script, and prompts whenever the model chains a command;
- environment quirks the model has to remember (the `GITHUB_TOKEN=` prefix);
- long command lines that break when copied ([#137](https://github.com/lokkju/skills/issues/137));
- the model parsing text output.

**Plan (decided 2026-11-04):** keep each CLI as the core, which people still run by hand, and add a thin MCP server over the same functions. Tools take and return JSON, with no shell, paths or quoting. Permissions go per tool: forge-tracker's reads are auto-allowed and its writes prompt. The same server works in Claude Code (a plugin `.mcp.json`) and OpenCode (its `mcp` config), which shrinks the per-harness layer.

**Pilot: forge-tracker.**
- An stdio server exposing the reads (catchup facts and report, next facts, save-slate, groom proposals, smoke) and the writes (groom apply, link, write) as separate tools.
- The skill and commands call the tools; the CLI stays for humans and for CI.
- Dependency choice: a small standard-library JSON-RPC-over-stdio layer, or `uv run --with mcp`. Pick the one that keeps installs simplest and say why.
- Measure against today's CLI route: tokens per /catchup and /next, permission prompts, failures.

**Then**, if the pilot pays off: worktree-tidy (listing stale worktrees; removal stays the user's) and term-recorder (run a step file, return snaps; replaces its `allowed-tools` grant). github-app-create stays a CLI, because it runs an interactive browser flow.

The hand-off check's unknown-label rule flagged `D123` in a reply that summarised [#129](https://github.com/lokkju/skills/issues/129). D123 belongs to the session that filed #129, not this one; the reply quoted it while describing the issue ("D123 sent a nested note about four-digit years instead of the item's actual recommendation"). The nudge then told the model to create D123 now, which would have queued a fake item under a new label.

**Expected:** a label that appears while describing an issue, PR, transcript or another session's queue isn't a hand-off. Options:
- skip a label in the same sentence or bullet as an issue/PR reference or link, or inside quotes and code;
- only flag labels at or above the session's current label range (this session was past D52, but D123 is above it, so range alone doesn't settle it; a label far above the next free number is more likely foreign);
- word the nudge as "if this is one of yours, create it; ignore it if it's quoted from elsewhere".

The existing rules from #108 already skip quotes and descriptions of output; this case is a description of another queue.

/catchup, /next and /groom ran against the session's working directory, which isn't always the project the session is about; /catchup then moved the wrong repo's marker.

Each command now opens with **First, which repo**. Before the first read, Claude works out the candidates from what the session did (repos it committed to or pushed, ran `git -C` in, opened or commented on PRs and issues in, or the user named), with the working directory as one candidate:

- only the working directory: run as before;
- one other candidate: say which repo it's reading, and run with `--repo <dir>` (later `labels`, `next facts`/`save-slate` and `groom apply` calls take the same `--repo`);
- several: ask which, offering each repo and "all of them". /catchup reads the chosen repos in one workspace-style report; /next and /groom run once per repo, since a slate and a numbered proposal list each belong to one repo;
- a run for Claude's own orientation (`--no-mark` for /catchup) settles it without asking.

CLI: `--repo` can now be given more than once. catchup reads exactly those repos, wherever they sit, as a workspace report (root is the folder they share, each repo keeps its own window and marker). Every other command refuses a second `--repo`, and it doesn't combine with `--workspace` or `--record`. Tests cover the multi-repo report, dedup, a chosen path that isn't a repo, and the refusals.

README and `docs/specs/forge-tracker.md` describe both.

Checked on screen with term-recorder: a throwaway Haiku session started in one repo (`infra`) made commits in two sibling repos with `git -C`, then ran `/forge-tracker:catchup` from `--plugin-dir`. It asked "Which repo should the catchup read?" offering alpha, beta, infra and "All of them" before any catchup read (no forge-tracker cache was written). A first run, before the "don't rule one out on a guess" sentence, had the model wrongly dismiss the sibling repos as having no remote and read the working directory; that sentence fixed it.

Closes #127

On lokkju/infra (forge-tracker 2026.10.15.3, `/next`, 2026-10-16), all 40 open issues came out `agent-ready` and "Your queue" was empty. The repo has no `labels.needs_human` or `labels.decision` configured and no issue carries a blocking link, so nothing could mark an item as anything but agent-ready. Many of them can't be done by an agent:

- **physical work:** lokkju/infra#136 (repaste a GPU), lokkju/infra#248 (pull a cable);
- **needs a device or a test window:** lokkju/infra#231, lokkju/infra#9, lokkju/infra#188;
- **research done, waiting on the owner's decision:** lokkju/infra#214, lokkju/infra#204, lokkju/infra#201, lokkju/infra#203, lokkju/infra#211, lokkju/infra#148;
- **waiting on an external event or another repo:** lokkju/infra#267, lokkju/infra#269, lokkju/infra#275, lokkju/infra#197, lokkju/infra#35, lokkju/infra#138;
- **explicitly parked:** lokkju/infra#305, whose title ends "(parked)".

So the classifier's default treats a missing signal as "an agent can do this". With no labels configured, that default is wrong for most real backlogs, and the report reads as a confident queue rather than an unclassified one. The ranking step then has to quietly leave such items out of lanes, which only the slate note records.

Expected (any of these would fix it):
1. **With no needs-human or decision label configured, classify `unknown` instead of `agent-ready`,** and say so in the report header. The facts schema already has `unknown`.
2. **Read cheap text signals** in the title and body: "(parked)", "blocked on", "waits on", "physically", "decision", "the operator decides", "after <issue>" with a link. Mark matches `unknown` or `person-only`, with the matched phrase as the marker.
3. **Let the slate move items into the queue.** `save-slate` accepts `overrules` only to move person-only items into lanes; it can't move an agent-ready item into "Your queue" with a reason. Allow the reverse overrule (item to queue, with a reason).

Also seen: the "Blocked" section said "nothing blocked" although several items name what they wait on in their body. With no blocking links that's technically true, but it should say "no blocking links recorded" rather than imply nothing is blocked.

